<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  How the archive turns into the .ipa that goes to App Store Connect.

  `release.sh` never passes this file to xcodebuild directly. It copies it into
  the output directory and writes the real Team ID into the copy, because the
  value below is a placeholder and a placeholder that reaches xcodebuild
  produces "exportArchive: No signing certificate iOS Distribution found",
  which sounds like a certificate problem and is not one.

  ── The one blank in this file ────────────────────────────────────────────
  teamID is the ten-character Team ID of the Pazl LLC Apple Developer account.
  Find it at https://developer.apple.com/account under Membership details, or
  in App Store Connect under Users and Access → Integrations. It looks like
  A1B2C3D4E5. Give it to the script as ALMA_TEAM_ID; do not edit this file.
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Why each key is what it is:

  · method — `app-store-connect` is the current spelling. Xcode 15.3 renamed
    the four methods (`app-store` → `app-store-connect`, `ad-hoc` → `release-testing`,
    `development` → `debugging`, `enterprise` → `enterprise`). The old names still
    work in Xcode 26 but print a deprecation notice, and the notice is the kind
    of thing that gets copied into a newer toolchain years later and then fails.

  · destination — `export` writes an .ipa to disk and stops. The alternative,
    `upload`, sends the build to App Store Connect from inside this command.
    Export is what this repository does, on purpose: uploading needs an App
    Store Connect API key or an Apple ID password, and no part of this project
    should ever be in a position to hold one. release.sh prints the upload
    command for a human to run.

  · signingStyle — automatic. Xcode asks the developer portal for a
    distribution certificate and an App Store profile for ai.pazl.alma and
    creates them if they are missing, which is why release.sh passes
    -allowProvisioningUpdates. Manual signing means checking a .mobileprovision
    into the repository or onto the build machine, and it expires every year at
    a moment nobody chose.

  · manageAppVersionAndBuildNumber — false, and this one matters. Left at its
    default of true, Xcode replaces CFBundleVersion with a number of its own
    choosing during export. The build number this project computes (minutes
    since 2026-01-01Z, see mobile/RELEASE.md) would then be silently discarded,
    and the number in TestFlight would not be the number in mobile/RELEASES.log.

  · uploadSymbols — true. Without the dSYM, every crash TestFlight and the
    Organizer show you is a column of hexadecimal. The archive contains it; this
    is what carries it along.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>method</key>
	<string>app-store-connect</string>

	<key>teamID</key>
	<string>TEAM_ID_GOES_HERE</string>

	<key>destination</key>
	<string>export</string>

	<key>signingStyle</key>
	<string>automatic</string>

	<key>manageAppVersionAndBuildNumber</key>
	<false/>

	<key>uploadSymbols</key>
	<true/>

	<key>stripSwiftSymbols</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
